            <ArticleTitle>Mismatch repair prefers exons.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1673-1674</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/ng.3993</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A new analysis of cancer genomes identifies a decrease in the mutation burden of exons, but not introns, as compared to expectation. This difference can be explained by preferential recruitment of the DNA mismatch repair machinery to a protein modification that marks exons.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
